  "notes": "The producer who imported R&B/hip-hop construction into country radio. The band is documented as a decision rather than a default: \"BPM 65–105 straight on 2&4, i.e. deliberately slower than the bro-country norm,\" with a signature production \"in the key of F major with a tempo of 96–100 beats per minute.\" Harmony is \"deliberately static: two-bar I–V–vi–IV loops under sustained pads, with the interest moved from chord change to rhythm and texture,\" which is why one family carries almost all the weight and `2_bars_per_chord` outweighs everything else. The kit is decided at source because he mixes his own productions, and the entry lists it: \"a snap/clap on the backbeat, 16th hats with occasional 32nd rolls, a short sampled kick on 1 and the 'and-of-3,' finger-snap and vocal-percussion layers, and live kit credited alongside 'programming' rather than instead of it. Model programming-as-skeleton with live cymbals/toms overdubbed.\" The 808 takes `counter_riff` because the entry places both layers: \"sub-bass as a structural element — long, tuned, root-locked, sometimes the only low end in a verse; an electric bass plays a syncopated R&B figure above it where present.\" Records are short and hook-first: \"2:30–3:00 … with a post-chorus instrumental figure and a bridge that strips to pad-and-vocal. Two-bar loop intros rather than four-bar band intros.\" ⚠ Confidence is medium and the research says why: the key/tempo, credited palette and producer-plus-mixer role are solid, \"his verbal workflow statements are not in reachable sources, so the drum specifics are read off the records and the credit list [LOW-CONF].\" ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the 808 register, the perc density and the counter ratio.",
